  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "The CDC recommends an annual flu shot for everyone 6 months and older, ideally by the end of October, to reduce the risk of infection, hospitalization, and severe illness. Protection takes about two weeks to develop, and vaccination remains beneficial even into the winter. It is particularly crucial for individuals at high risk, including those over 65, pregnant women, and people with chronic health conditions. Key Information About Flu Shots When to Get Vaccinated: The CDC advises getting vaccinated by the end of October. However,, getting vaccinated later, even in January or beyond, is still beneficial as long as the virus is circulating. Who Should Get It: Annual vaccination is recommended for everyone aged 6 months and older. High-Risk Groups: Individuals at higher risk of complications—including seniors (65+), young children, pregnant women, and those with chronic diseases (e.g., asthma, diabetes)—should prioritize getting the vaccine. Effectiveness: While effectiveness can vary, the vaccine is designed to protect against the three or four influenza viruses likely to circulate each season. Even if you still get the flu, studies show it can reduce the severity of illness and risk of hospitalization. Types of Vaccines: Most vaccines are administered via needle, with some options available for individuals 18-64 to receive them via jet injector. Vaccine Safety: Flu vaccines cannot cause the flu. COVID-19 Interaction: Flu shots can be given at the same time as a COVID-19 vaccine. Cost: Flu shots are generally covered at no cost by Medicare Part B and most private insurance plans. Where to Get a ShotFlu shots are widely available at pharmacies (e.g., CVS, Walmart), doctor's offices, health clinics, and sometimes workplaces or schools.",
